def longest_palindrome(s: str) -> str:
    if not s:
        return ""

    def expand(left: int, right: int):
        while left >= 0 and right < len(s) and s[left] == s[right]:
            left -= 1
            right += 1
        return left + 1, right  # start index (inclusive), end index (exclusive)

    best_start, best_end = 0, 1  # at least one character

    for i in range(len(s)):
        # Odd-length palindromes centered at i
        l, r = expand(i, i)
        if r - l > best_end - best_start:
            best_start, best_end = l, r
        # Even-length palindromes centered between i and i+1
        l, r = expand(i, i + 1)
        if r - l > best_end - best_start:
            best_start, best_end = l, r

    return s[best_start:best_end]
```

This uses the expand-around-center approach: for each possible center (2n - 1 centers total), it expands outward while the substring remains a palindrome, running in O(n²) time and O(1) extra space. Because it scans centers left to right and only updates the best when a strictly longer palindrome is found, ties are resolved in favor of the earliest-starting palindrome.

